The mechanism is not entropy per se but the interaction of entropy with model capability. In the intermediate-SC region, high entropy reflects genuine uncertainty where the model has partial but not dominant knowledge; constraints can tip the balance. Outside this region, high entropy signals either rare failures (Type~A) or pervasive confusion (Type~C), and constraints amplify rather than correct. The aggregate $\Delta$ is determined by the proportion of high-entropy problems and the sign of their contribution, which explains why the effect is regime-dependent (\S\ref{sec:main-results}).

\subsection{Constraint Ablation}
\label{sec:ablation}

To test whether the Z3 solver contributes beyond simple vote counting, we compare four scoring methods on Qwen2.5-14B across FOLIO and ProofWriter ($n = 804$). MAX-SAT achieves 76.49\% (615/804). Count-only (sum constraint weights per candidate without invoking Z3) achieves 76.62\% (616/804). Random-weight MAX-SAT achieves 75.87\% (610/804). The SC baseline achieves 76.24\% (613/804). MAX-SAT and count-only agree on 803 of 804 predictions. The Z3 solver adds nothing over simple counting. This confirms the degeneration in Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}: although ${\sim}$92\% of constraints are technically discriminative (\S\ref{sec:extraction-taxonomy}), their discriminative direction mirrors the majority answer, so the MAX-SAT objective reduces to a weighted vote count and SICA collapses to SC.

\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

We test twelve remediation strategies: nine prompt-level spanning six intervention dimensions (full prompt-level results in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:remediation}) and three method-level targeting different aspects of the independence bottleneck (Table~\ref{tab:method_interventions}). \emph{Prompting}: contrastive prompting reduces the non-discriminative rate from 8.2\% to 6.0\% but the newly discriminative constraints still mirror the majority answer ($\Delta = -1.1$\,pp); filtering to remove non-discriminative constraints yields zero gain. \emph{Architecture}: cross-model extraction fails in both directions. When a weaker model (Mistral-7B) extracts from stronger traces (Qwen2.5-14B), it produces 0\% discriminative constraints ($\Delta = 0$\,pp). In the reverse direction, stronger models (Qwen3-14B, Qwen2.5-14B) extracting from Mistral-7B FOLIO traces produce accuracy within $\pm$1\,pp of self-extraction (60.29\% and 58.33\% vs.\ 59.31\%), confirming that the bottleneck is in trace content, not extractor capability. \emph{Capability}: generational scaling with Qwen3-14B yields $\Delta = -0.49$\,pp (n.s.). \emph{Verification}: Z3 feedback loops trigger UNSAT on only 0.9\% of problems with no real accuracy impact; cross-trace UNSAT checking finds 56.4\% of constraint pairs share zero variables, so logical verification is vacuous. \emph{Diversity}: only 1 of 4 temperature settings ($T = 0.7$) yields a positive SICA effect ($p = 0.092$, marginal); $T \in \{0.3, 0.5, 1.0\}$ all produce negative or null effects ($\Delta \in [-1.47, 0.00]$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:t-sensitivity}); multi-temperature sampling ($n = 204$) produces $\Delta = 0.00$\,pp (SC $= 57.84\%$, SICA $= 57.84\%$). \emph{Filtering}: consistency filtering (retaining constraints in $\geq 4$ traces) prunes aggressively (1.8 constraints per problem at threshold 4, 0.3 at threshold 8) and reverts to SC. All six dimensions fail. The cross-model results are particularly striking in both directions: when a weaker model extracts from stronger traces, discriminative rate drops to 0\%; when stronger models extract from weaker traces, accuracy stays within $\pm$1\,pp of self-extraction. Neither direction breaks the bottleneck because the constraint content is determined by the traces, not the extractor. Only oracle constraints (completely independent of the traces) produce large gains. These nine prompt-level strategies confirm that the independence bottleneck cannot be resolved by prompt engineering, solver refinements, or diversity manipulations alone.

\paragraph{Method-level interventions.} Three method-level strategies target different aspects of the independence bottleneck on Mistral-7B (Table~\ref{tab:method_interventions}). Independent Constraint Generation (ICG) bypasses trace-constraint coupling by generating constraints directly from the problem statement. Across two FOLIO seeds, ICG produces $\Delta = -1.96$\,pp (seed 42) and $+1.47$\,pp (seed 123); on LogiQA, $\Delta = -0.50$\,pp. The near-zero average indicates that model bias originates in problem understanding, not in trace-constraint coupling. Contrastive scoring generates pro and con constraints for each candidate answer. On FOLIO, this yields $\Delta = -9.31$\,pp with 20.1\% degenerate scores (identical pro and con totals). The pro/con imbalance reflects the model's prior rather than balanced evidence evaluation. Debate adds a proposer-critic-defense chain before constraint extraction. On FOLIO, $\Delta = -18.63$\,pp, the largest degradation across all twelve strategies. At the 7B scale, multi-role prompting adds cognitive overhead that degrades both reasoning quality and constraint extraction. All three method-level strategies fail, confirming that the bottleneck extends beyond prompt engineering to the self-extraction paradigm itself.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Method-level interventions on Mistral-7B. Each targets a different aspect of the independence bottleneck. All produce neutral-to-catastrophic results. ICG shows two FOLIO seeds; LogiQA results for Contrastive and Debate are pending.}
\label{tab:method_interventions}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrr@{}}
\toprule
Method & Target & FOLIO $\Delta$ (pp) & LogiQA $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
SC baseline & --- & 0.00 & 0.00 \\
ICG & Trace coupling & $-$1.96 / $+$1.47 & $-$0.50 \\
Contrastive & Scoring bias & $\mathbf{-9.31}$ & --- \\
Debate & Role diversity & $\mathbf{-18.63}$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Independence-accuracy trade-off.} The failure pattern becomes interpretable through $\kappa$ generalization analysis. Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization} compares Fleiss' $\kappa$ across three aggregation methods on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$). Self-Consistency produces $\kappa = 0.44$ (Eff-$K = 2.02$, nominal $K = 10$). Diverse-Prompt, which varies the instruction prompt across traces, achieves $\kappa = 0.40$ (Eff-$K = 2.21$, nominal $K = 12$): prompt variation does not break the underlying correlation. Debate achieves $\kappa = 0.15$ (Eff-$K = 4.44$), nearly tripling the effective sample size, but collapses accuracy by $-18.6$\,pp. This constitutes a fundamental trade-off: methods that increase trace independence (Debate) destroy the accuracy that makes aggregation useful, while methods that preserve accuracy (ICG, Diverse-Prompt) cannot break the correlation floor. The trade-off is a Pareto frontier inherent to same-model aggregation, not an engineering gap. The only demonstrated path past this frontier requires signal from outside the model's own distribution, consistent with the oracle result (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) and with published systems that bypass self-extraction \citep{xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, pan2023logiclm}.

